WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.830
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Fer.

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:04.595
FER OLIVEIRA: Hi, Martin.

00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:06.720
MARTIN OMANDER: Fer, you
are a principal serverless

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.770
specialist.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.870
I've heard that means that
you spend a lot of time

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.600
with businesses who are
moving to the cloud?

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.400
FER OLIVEIRA: I sure do.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.130
MARTIN OMANDER: I'd love to hear
if they know about serverless,

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.650
and if so, what
their thoughts are.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:21.070
FER OLIVEIRA: Happy to share.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.392 --> 00:00:31.850
MARTIN OMANDER:
All right, I really

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:35.030
enjoy building serverless
apps with Cloud Run.

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:39.860
But do engineering managers and
CTOs know what serverless is?

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.170
FER OLIVEIRA: Well, they
look at the bigger picture.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:45.170
The development process
passes through several stages,

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.290
before software is delivered
to their customers.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.060
They think about how
to make each step

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.940
cost-effective and predictable.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:55.380
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
that makes sense.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.920
FER OLIVEIRA: Let's start with
the first step, provisioning.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:00.470
It can be a lot of work
to set up all [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.620
machines needed for an
enterprise application.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:07.790
And CTOs know that their bosses
get upset if they get it wrong.

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:10.970
If they overprovision, the
business is paying too much.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.370
If they underprovision,
the application

00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:14.750
doesn't run properly.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.620
One way to make this step
easier and less risky

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.740
is to go serverless
with Cloud Run.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:25.400
Cloud Run scales up
and down with traffic,

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.260
and setup is easy.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.020
CTOs like that predictability.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.530
MARTIN OMANDER: I can see that.

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:34.830
What about the second
step in your diagram?

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:38.130
As a developer, I
really enjoy that step.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.350
FER OLIVEIRA: Many CTOs
and engineering managers

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.810
don't enjoy it as
much as you do.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:46.620
For them, this step
is often a bottleneck.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.990
It's hard for them to
find good developers

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.370
with the right skills--

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:54.640
coding ability, Kubernetes
experience, Linux operations,

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.725
et cetera.

00:01:55.725 --> 00:01:56.600
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:57.590
Well, that sounds hard.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.750
I mean, I don't
know all that stuff.

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:01.430
FER OLIVEIRA: Right,
but there is a way

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.900
to streamline this step.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.780
Instead of trying to find a
team of all-around developer

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.919
superstars, many CTOs focus on
picking a platform that makes

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:12.320
regular developers productive.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:15.680
Serverless platforms
like Cloud Run help here.

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.190
Developers who know one or
two programming languages

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:20.570
can be productive.

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:23.270
They don't need to be
programmers and IT-operations

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:23.885
experts.

00:02:23.885 --> 00:02:25.760
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, good
to know that there's

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.340
a place for regular
developers in the world.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.430
OK, what about the release step?

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:34.490
FER OLIVEIRA: In this step,
CTOs and engineering managers

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.800
are looking for
increased automation

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.090
to achieve faster
time to market.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:43.220
One way to do this is to use
build packs instead of Docker.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.010
These reduce the operational
burden on developers,

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:49.160
while supporting enterprise
operators who manage

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:51.230
apps at scale.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:52.730
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, I know Docker,

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.400
but I use build packs
all the time, anyway.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.617
It means fewer moving
parts in my application.

00:02:58.617 --> 00:02:59.450
FER OLIVEIRA: Right.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.160
Build packs can
also make it easier

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:04.280
to meet security and compliance
requirements without developer

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:05.090
intervention.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.280
With Cloud Run, you
can choose whether you

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.820
want to use Docker files
or build in build packs.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.160
If you use build packs,
you can go straight

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.560
from source code to production.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.240
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
and then there's

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.598
the run step in your diagram.

00:03:20.598 --> 00:03:22.140
FER OLIVEIRA:
Enterprise applications

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.630
are often complex and
hard to keep running.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.150
CTOs know that the
business loses money

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.190
when the applications go down.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.170
For example, they might
lose their ability

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.990
to talk to their
customers, to take orders,

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:35.670
to charge their customers.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.780
Or their employees may be forced
to sit idle when enterprise

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:41.720
application goes down.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:42.520
MARTIN OMANDER: Hm.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:45.590
How do they prevent
those outages?

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:47.420
FER OLIVEIRA: CTOs
know that complexity

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.130
is the enemy in this step.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.680
One way to reduce
complexity is to let

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.720
your cloud provider handle it.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.660
Cloud Run does this
by abstracting away

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:57.800
the infrastructure.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.710
And it's fully integrated
with cloud monitoring

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:05.135
and cloud logging for an
enhanced operator experience.

00:04:05.135 --> 00:04:07.760
MARTIN OMANDER: All right, thank
you very much for sharing this

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:08.840
with us today, Fer.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.800
And thank you,
everyone, for watching.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.410
If you have questions
for me or Fer,

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.310
please add them in the comments.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.770
Also, write in the
comments if you

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.050
have topics for other
serverless things

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.430
we should talk
about in the show.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:21.289
Thank you for today.

00:04:21.289 --> 00:04:24.639
[MUSIC PLAYING]

